\chapter{Progress and the grade sent to Canvas}
\label{Progress}

FBF grades an activity on completion: a student who watched the video and
wrote the comments asked for gets the full grade, one who watched half of it
and wrote one comment gets part of it.
Canvas receives a single number per student, and neither the student nor
the teacher sees there how it came about.
FBF's own view shows the parts, but one student at a time.
The [[progress]] command lists, for every student and FBF assignment, the
score FBF computes, whether FBF has sent it to Canvas, the score Canvas
has, and the parts the score is made of.

That answers the questions a teacher gets: why do I have 7 of 10 points,
why is my grade not in Canvas yet, why does Canvas say something else than
FBF.
The last two have options of their own, [[--unpublished]] and
[[--mismatch]], which leave only the students concerned.


\section{How FBF computes the grade}

We learned the model by exploring a course with sixty activities, and
verified the formula on all of its 9\,468 progresses.
\Cref{progress-model} shows the resources involved.
\begin{description}
\item[Tasks and facets.] An activity consists of tasks, such as
  watching a video, writing a reflection, submitting a file or reviewing
  peers.
  Each kind has a type of its own: [[multimedia-tasks]],
  [[reflect-tasks]], [[submit-tasks]], [[submission-review-tasks]] and so
  on.
  A task is graded on one or more \emph{facets}, such as [[viewed]] or
  [[comments]].
  For every facet~$F$ the task has an attribute [[F-facet-weight]], the
  points it is worth.
  The weights of all tasks in an activity add up to 100.
  Facets that count something, such as comments, also have
  [[F-facet-amount]], the number required for the full weight.
  A task's [[order]] is its place in the activity; tasks have no titles of
  their own.
\item[Progress.] Each student has one [[progresses]] resource per activity.
  Its attribute [[grades]] maps each task id to the points earned per
  facet, and [[grades.overall]] is the grade out of 100.
  A task the student never touched has no entry.
  Its [[metadata]] maps task ids to what the student did, such as the
  number of comments; it is [[null]] for a student who did nothing.
  [[progress]] is the percentage of the activity completed, which FBF shows
  next to the grade.
\item[Grade sent.] When FBF sends the grade to Canvas, it sets the
  progress's [[grades-published-at]] and links the progress through
  [[latest-grade]] to an [[lti/grade]], whose [[grade]] is the overall grade
  divided by 100.
  Canvas scores it as that fraction of the assignment's points.
\item[Who.] The progress's [[actor]] is an FBF user, of type [[users]] or,
  for some students who never opened the activity, [[people]].
  FBF users carry no Canvas or LTI id, so the only key to Canvas is the
  email address, which [[v1/people/<actor>/emails]] gives for both types.
\end{description}

The points of a facet are its weight times the fraction completed: all of
it for a boolean facet like [[viewed]], and the count over the amount,
capped at one, for counting facets (this held for all but three of some
1\,750 comment facets, which we cannot explain).
The overall grade is the sum of the points of all facets, on every
progress we checked, if we leave out one kind of key: the [[grades]] of a
multimedia task also have a key per teacher question,
[[open-question-<id>]] and the like, which is always 0 and has no weight.
The grade sent is the overall grade over 100 at the time of sending, and
the score in Canvas is the grade sent times the assignment's points, for
every grade sent we checked.
So the score in Canvas should be the overall grade times the points over
100, unless the student did more after FBF sent the grade (three did) or
someone changed the score in Canvas.

