\section{From FBF users to Canvas students}

FBF knows its users by email address only, so that is how we find them in
Canvas.
Canvas has the address as the user's [[email]], and at KTH also as the
[[login_id]]; we accept either, in any case, since the two systems need not
agree on capitals.
An actor may have several addresses, and any of them may match.

<<functions>>=
def match_actors(emails, students):
    """Map FBF actor ids to Canvas `students` by email address.

    `emails` maps actor ids to sets of addresses.  Actors without a match,
    such as teachers, are left out.
    """
    by_address = {}
    for student in students:
        for key in ("email", "login_id"):
            address = getattr(student, key, None)
            if address:
                by_address.setdefault(address.lower(), student)
    matched = {}
    for actor, addresses in emails.items():
        for address in sorted(address.lower() for address in addresses):
            if address in by_address:
                matched[actor] = by_address[address]
                break
    return matched
@

The matching is case insensitive on both sides and takes either Canvas
field, an address that matches nobody (an old one, say) does not stop the
next from matching, and an actor that is not a student (here a teacher)
is not matched.

<<test functions>>=
STUDENTS = [SimpleNamespace(id=1, login_id="ada@example.com",
                            email="ada@example.com"),
            SimpleNamespace(id=2, login_id="Bo@Example.com", email=None),
            SimpleNamespace(id=3, login_id=None, email="cy@example.com")]

def test_match_actors_by_email_or_login():
  matched = match_actors({"a1": {"Ada@Example.com"},
                          "a2": {"bo@example.com"},
                          "a3": {"alumni@example.com", "cy@example.com"},
                          "teacher": {"t@example.com"}}, STUDENTS)
  assert {actor: s.id for actor, s in matched.items()} \
    == {"a1": 1, "a2": 2, "a3": 3}
