\subsection{Selecting students}

Two questions come up often enough to deserve an option each.
\begin{description}
\item[Why is my grade not in Canvas?] FBF publishes grades when the teacher
  or a deadline says so, not as students work.
  [[--unpublished]] keeps the students who have earned something that FBF
  has not published.
\item[Why does Canvas say something else?] FBF sends a grade when it
  publishes; if the student does more afterwards, or a teacher changes the
  score in Canvas, the two drift apart.
  [[--mismatch]] keeps the students whose Canvas score differs from FBF's
  score.
  A Canvas score for a student without progress is one, as FBF would
  give 0.
  A student without a Canvas score is one only if FBF has published a
  grade: then Canvas lost the score or never got it, which we found for
  63 students in four assignments of our course, all with grades FBF
  reported as sent.
  Without a published grade, the missing score is what
  [[--unpublished]] is for.
\end{description}
Given both, a row must meet both, as with other filters.

<<functions>>=
def unpublished(row):
    """Whether `row` has points that FBF has not published."""
    return (row.overall or 0) > 0 and not row.published


def mismatch(row):
    """Whether the Canvas score of `row` differs from FBF's score.

    A missing Canvas score differs only from a published FBF grade.
    """
    if row.canvas_score is None:
        return row.published
    return abs(row.canvas_score - (row.fbf_score or 0)) > TOLERANCE
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_filters():
  def row(**fields):
    return Progress("W", 10, "s", 1, **fields)
  assert unpublished(row(overall=10.0))
  assert not unpublished(row(overall=10.0, published=True))
  assert not unpublished(row(overall=0.0))
  assert not unpublished(row())
  assert mismatch(row(fbf_score=6.0, canvas_score=5.0))
  assert not mismatch(row(fbf_score=6.0, canvas_score=6.004))
  assert not mismatch(row(fbf_score=6.0))
  assert mismatch(row(fbf_score=6.0, published=True))
  assert mismatch(row(canvas_score=3.0))
@


\section{Fetching}
\label{progress-fetch}

The FBF side of an assignment takes four kinds of request, the Canvas side
two.
The tasks come with the activity, through [[include=tasks]], since the
activity has them as bare references (\cref{API}).

<<functions>>=
def activity_tasks(fbf, activity_id):
    """Return the tasks of the activity `activity_id`."""
    document = fbf.get(f"v1/activities/{activity_id}", include="tasks")
    references = relationship(document.resource, "tasks").get("data") or []
    return [document.included[(reference["type"], reference["id"])]
            for reference in references]
@

The progresses of an activity are one request, [[v1/progresses]] with the
activity's id as a parameter.
FBF ignores parameters it does not support and then returns all
progresses the user can see, which would mix activities silently.
We therefore keep only the progresses of this activity.

<<functions>>=
def activity_progresses(fbf, activity_id):
    """Return the progresses of the students in the activity."""
    return [progress for progress in
            fbf.get("v1/progresses", activity_id=activity_id).resources
            if related_id(progress, "progressable") == activity_id]
@

A recording cannot make FBF ignore the parameter, so we let a client that
returns everything stand in for FBF (as for [[get_by_ids]] in \cref{API}):
the progress of another activity must not get through.

<<test functions>>=
from fbf.api import Document

class EverythingFBF:
  def get(self, path, **params):
    other = progress("a9")
    other["relationships"]["progressable"]["data"]["id"] = "elsewhere"
    return Document({"data": [progress("a1"), other]})

def test_activity_progresses_keeps_only_the_activity():
  found = activity_progresses(EverythingFBF(), "act")
  assert [related_id(p, "actor") for p in found] == ["a1"]
@

The grades sent are fetched by the ids that the progresses point to.
The email addresses are the expensive part: one request per student, since
FBF offers no list of them.
A course's activities have mostly the same students, so we fetch each
actor's addresses once per run and keep them in [[known]], which the
command passes from assignment to assignment.

<<functions>>=
def sent_grades(fbf, progresses):
    """Map the `lti/grade` ids of `progresses` to the grades sent."""
    return fbf.get_by_ids("v1/lti/grades",
                          [related_id(progress, "latest-grade")
                           for progress in progresses])


def actor_emails(fbf, actors, known):
    """Add the email addresses of `actors` to `known`; return `known`.

    `known` maps actor ids to sets of addresses.
    """
    missing = sorted(set(actors) - set(known))
    documents = fbf.get_many([(f"v1/people/{actor}/emails", {})
                              for actor in missing])
    for actor, document in zip(missing, documents):
        known[actor] = {attribute(email, "address")
                        for email in document.resources
                        if attribute(email, "address")}
    return known
@

On the Canvas side we need the course's students, with their email
addresses, which Canvas gives only when asked, and the assignment's scores.
The students we fetch once per course, like the addresses.

We cannot leave the choice of students to Canvas.
The Canvas object that [[canvaslms]] hands the command caches a course's
users and returns all of them, whatever [[enrollment_type]] says; in our
course that added two dozen teaching assistants, who then got rows of
their own.
[[canvaslms]]'s own commands filter by the users' enrollments, and so do
we: we ask for the enrollments and keep the users with a student
enrollment.
A user without enrollments we drop, since then we cannot tell.

<<functions>>=
def course_students(canvas, course_id, known):
    """Return the students of a Canvas course, from `known` if there."""
    if course_id not in known:
        course = canvas.get_course(course_id)
        known[course_id] = students_only(course.get_users(
            enrollment_type=["student"], include=["email", "enrollments"]))
    return known[course_id]


def students_only(users):
    """Return the Canvas `users` that have a student enrollment."""
    return [user for user in users
            if any(enrollment.get("type") == "StudentEnrollment"
                   for enrollment in getattr(user, "enrollments", None)
                                     or [])]


def canvas_scores(assignment):
    """Map Canvas user ids to their scores in `assignment`."""
    return {submission.user_id: submission.score
            for submission in assignment.get_submissions()}
@

The users are shaped like [[canvasapi]]'s, whose [[enrollments]] are plain
dictionaries; a user with two enrollments, one of them as a student,
counts as a student.

<<test functions>>=
def test_students_only_keeps_student_enrollments():
  def user(id, *types):
    return SimpleNamespace(id=id, enrollments=[{"type": t} for t in types])
  users = [user(1, "StudentEnrollment"), user(2, "TaEnrollment"),
           user(3, "TaEnrollment", "StudentEnrollment"), user(4),
           SimpleNamespace(id=5)]
  assert [u.id for u in students_only(users)] == [1, 3]
@

[[assignment_progress]] puts the pieces together for one assignment and
warns about what it leaves out or cannot explain.
The warnings give counts, not names: they go to standard error, where they
end up in logs that should hold no personal data.

<<imports>>=
import sys
<<functions>>=
def assignment_progress(fbf, canvas, assignment, activity_id, emails,
                        students):
    """Return the `Progress` rows of a Canvas `assignment`.

    `activity_id` is the assignment's FBF activity; `emails` and `students`
    are caches as in `actor_emails` and `course_students`.
    """
    tasks = activity_tasks(fbf, activity_id)
    progresses = activity_progresses(fbf, activity_id)
    actors = {related_id(progress, "actor") for progress in progresses}
    actor_emails(fbf, actors, emails)
    members = course_students(canvas, assignment.course_id, students)
    student_of = match_actors({actor: emails[actor] for actor in actors},
                              members)
    sent = sent_grades(fbf, progresses)
    rows = assemble(assignment, tasks, progresses, sent, student_of,
                    members, canvas_scores(assignment))
    <<warn about actors left out and grades not explained>>
    return rows
@

<<warn about actors left out and grades not explained>>=
